  "claim_scope": "v8 (Opus P29 NEW-25, the P1): S29 changed the Python/Rust bound-parity test without a successor packet, so this lineage pinned the parent bytes and the repository hygiene gate was red at P29 while the packet replay stayed green; this packet pins the repaired bytes. NEW-23: the recursive-scan pin now asserts on the file list the scan consumed (rust_files), so reverting rglob to glob fails the test instead of a layout property that holds either way. The transfer transition re-validates its inputs at entry (NEW-24), which does not change any bound. Earlier: v7 (Opus P26 NEW-17): the total-parity docstring says what the scan does (every .rs under src/ recursively) instead of the wording that hid the P25 survivor; bytes-only doc move. Earlier: v6 (Opus P25 NEW-13): the crate-wide parity scan recurses (rglob), so a bound declared in a crate subdirectory can no longer escape the total mapping; the packet's outside-canonical mutation row has no surviving member. Earlier: v5 (PR #532 incorporation + Opus P24): the transition row-ceiling boundary is TOTALISED: both transitions return the closed POST_STATE_RESOURCE_BOUND_EXCEEDED reject before oversized post-state construction (the PR's four Python boundary cases plus a compiled Rust replay supersede the two raise-pinning tests, which are deleted and their killer rows re-pointed); bound parity becomes CRATE-WIDE (every pub const MAX_ across src/*.rs, 37 bounds, 13 new twins) and both parity tests reject duplicate declarations so a comment-masked value drift cannot last-win (Opus P24 NEW-12). Earlier: v4 (C8''''''', Opus P23 NEW-11): the parity regex requires pub/const as separate tokens with any whitespace and admits any type spelling (primitive, CamelCase alias, path-qualified), so mutation row 6's stated class has no surviving member (the reviewer's five survivors plus the alias and path forms are all captured and fail the key-set equality). Earlier: v3 (C8'''''', Opus P22 repairs): the total-parity regex drops its integer-type alternation and tolerates any spacing, so TOTAL is true as written (NEW-7, the surviving u32/i64/spacing mutants are killed); the transfer boundary match pins the exact ceiling message, not a prefix shared with the ordering reject (NEW-9); the managed-lifecycle ceiling claim is bound by its own authorised-issue boundary test (NEW-10). Earlier: v2 (C8''''', Opus P21 NEW-4/NEW-6): parity over canonical.rs becomes TOTAL: one test enumerates every pub const MAX_ bound (usize/u64/u128, expression-valued included) and requires each to resolve through a single Python mapping with an equal value, failing on any unlisted name; the shared non-total row-ceiling boundary of transition_asset_transfer_v1 is pinned by a construction boundary test at exactly the balance ceiling, and both transition modules document the ceiling as an ABI decode bound (typed-reject totalisation deferred to lane work). The two transition modules join the pinned surface. Earlier: Research-only evidence that GlobalSettlementABI V1 Python effect plans and Python and Rust global states enforce matched finite collection ceilings before item traversal. Exact-limit inputs proceed to semantic validation, maximum-plus-one inputs reject at the bound, and below-limit canonical bytes and roots are unchanged.",
